PART VIII — ARCHITECTURE AND SAFETY

30. Integrate With the Existing Modules

Prefer focused extensions.

Suggested ownership:

situation.js
Richer derived match and risk context, using real state and perceived information appropriately.

strategy.js
Higher-level objectives, contextual mode selection, long-term decisions, and objective persistence.

stage-model.js
Position quality, safe destinations, route awareness, and live stage hazards.

values.js / evaluator.js
Strategic utility, position value, engagement-versus-disengagement evaluation, and match consequences.

opponent-model.js
Additional observed behavioral patterns where justified.

brain.js
Coordinating objective selection, tactical plans, survival priorities, interruption, and reassessment.

plans.js
Purposeful waiting, retreating, defensive holding, and other multi-step goals.

defense.js
Threat response integrated with strategic retreat and resource management.

telemetry.js
Explainability and measurement of newly introduced behaviors.

difficulty.js
Careful balancing and any justified new profile dimensions.

Do not force this exact structure if a cleaner solution fits the current code better.

Avoid creating a monolithic decision function.

Keep the system modular, testable, and maintainable.

31. Preserve Information Fairness

CPU Intelligence must not cheat.

Continue using the current perception pipeline for opponent information.

Do not directly consult hidden future actions or raw player inputs.

Do not use unannounced stage hazard outcomes.

Do not let CPUs instantly recognize all actions regardless of difficulty.

Public match facts such as score and remaining time can be known accurately.

Opponent intentions, precise future movement, and unobserved actions must remain uncertain.

Higher difficulty should improve inference and decision-making, not grant supernatural knowledge.

32. Preserve Determinism and Performance

The game uses fixed-step simulation.

Keep all AI behavior deterministic for the same initial state, seed, and inputs.

Use the existing seeded RNG.

Do not introduce wall-clock-based decisions.

Avoid excessive allocations, expensive full-stage searches every frame, or unnecessarily repeated simulations.

Cache suitable computations.

Use different update frequencies for high-level strategy and urgent tactical responses when appropriate.

Do not delay emergency recovery or defensive reactions merely to preserve a strategic update interval.

Performance must remain suitable for desktop and mobile browsers.

33. Avoid Regressions

Preserve:

* All existing fighter attacks and mechanics.
* Move coverage at every difficulty.
* Shield and Deflect functionality.
* Launch steering.
* Offstage recovery.
* Stage navigation.
* Projectile interactions.
* Combos.
* Cooldowns and Energy rules.
* CPU-versus-CPU Watch Mode.
* Quick Battle behavior.
* Deterministic tests.
* Fighter universality.
* Legitimate emergency threat responses.

Practice Ground should retain its intended training dummy behavior.

Do not modify character_rule.

Do not change movement, damage, launch, Energy, cooldowns, hitboxes, or other shared combat mechanics merely to create difficulty differences.

Any such unrelated change should be treated as out of scope.

